<div class="home-grid">
<section class="home-section">
  <h2>What it is</h2>
  <p>
    Flim is a <a href="https://matrix.org/">Matrix</a> messenger (based on
    <a href="https://element.io/">Element&nbsp;X</a>)
    where voice messages get titles and summaries. Compared with other Matrix clients,
    you get titles and summaries for your voice messages, and an easier way to navigate between chats.
    <br />
    As of what Matrix is: compared with e.g. WhatsApp or Telegram, it's an end-to-end encrypted
    messaging network where you can use different clients, and with Matrix you’re
    not locked to one company. More on the <a href="/about">about page</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Voice messages are great until you’re scrolling a wall of identical bubbles
    trying to remember which one was the plan and which one was the grocery list.
    Titles (and a bit of context) make that pile navigable again.
  </p>
  <p>
    And a lot of “most common” chat actions still assume two hands or a long
    reach to the top of the screen. Flim tries to put the exits and controls you
    actually use where a thumb can find them.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
  <h2>Standing on Element X</h2>
  <p>
    Flim is a modified version of <a href="https://element.io/">Element&nbsp;X</a> —
    the open-source <a href="https://matrix.org/">Matrix</a> client from Element.
    Encryption help, device verification, and a lot of the hard plumbing
    still live in that world; we link out to Element’s docs from our
    <a href="/help">help</a> pages instead of pretending we rewrote everything.
    Flim’s own source is on the
    <a href="/source">source</a>
    page.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="home-section">
  <h2>Before you dive in</h2>
  <p>
    Policies are still drafts — honest about what we know, not dressed up as
    lawyer-stamped gospel. Skim
    <a href="/privacy">privacy</a>,
    <a href="/acceptable-use">acceptable use</a>, and
    <a href="/copyright">copyright</a> when you care; they’re written in plain
    language on purpose.
  </p>
</section>
